Takayasu's arteritis
Rheumatology & Immunology
Upper extremity claudication, Absent radial pulses, Asymmetric blood pressures

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-047
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe, aching pain in the left arm when attempting to use it, combined with profound dizziness and blurred vision when turning the head, prompting fears of an impending stroke.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient experiencing severe arm claudication and alarming neurological symptoms (lightheadedness, visual changes) related to head position. Urgent after-hours bedside assessment was absolutely critical to physically evaluate upper extremity perfusion, assess for critical carotid/vertebral artery stenosis, and arrange immediate emergency transfer, as waiting until standard hours carried an imminent, life-threatening risk of a massive ischemic stroke or limb ischemia.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My left arm feels completely dead and aches terribly when I try to lift anything, and the room spins and my vision goes black if I turn my head quickly."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 3-month history of low-grade fever, night sweats, non-specific arthralgias, and severe fatigue. Over the past 48 hours, they developed severe claudication of the upper extremities, specifically characterized by intense left arm pain, heaviness, and rapid fatigue with minimal use (e.g., brushing hair). Today, the patient experienced alarming neurological symptoms: profound lightheadedness, visual disturbances (transient blurring), and near-syncope, which are explicitly triggered by turning the head to the left. The patient also complains of a new, dull, aching pain in the neck.
Functional Status: Resting strictly in a chair; refusing to move the head or use the left arm due to the terrifying visual symptoms and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme risk of imminent major stroke or syncope resulting in a severe fall; spouse is highly distressed.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: Right Arm 160/90 mmHg | Left Arm 95/60 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 98% (Right hand)
General: Patient is pale, sitting rigidly, and moving the head extremely slowly and deliberately.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular/Vascular: There is a massive, clinically significant discrepancy in systolic blood pressure between the arms (160 vs 95 mmHg). The left radial and brachial pulses are entirely absent to palpation ('pulseless disease'). The right radial pulse is strong. Loud, harsh bruits are clearly audible over the left subclavian artery and the bilateral carotid arteries. HEENT: Carotid artery tenderness (carotodynia) is elicited upon gentle palpation of the neck. Neurological: Cranial nerves intact at rest. No focal motor or sensory deficits in the lower limbs. The symptoms of near-syncope on head turning indicate critical vertebrobasilar insufficiency. The diagnostic tetrad is unequivocally present: classic upper extremity claudication, absent radial pulses, and massive asymmetric blood pressures, predicting long-segment narrowing/occlusion of the aortic arch branches on urgent hospital CT/MR angiography.
Environmental Observations: Clear path to the door; patient advised not to stand abruptly.
Medication Reconciliation: No current cardiovascular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Takayasu's arteritis - Status: Exacerbated (Acute critical cranial and upper limb ischemia)
Differentials:

Acute Arterial Embolism (Left Arm) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a chronic prodrome (fever, sweats), the bilateral carotid bruits, and the vertebrobasilar symptoms, which point to a diffuse inflammatory disease of the aortic arch rather than a solitary, isolated clot.

Giant Cell Arteritis (Temporal Arteritis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound, massive blood pressure discrepancy in the arms and the absent radial pulse (classic for Takayasu's arch involvement), though both are large vessel vasculitides and treated similarly in the acute phase.

Severe Atherosclerotic Peripheral Artery Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The patient's relatively young age (typical for Takayasu's), the prominent systemic inflammatory prodrome, and the specific "pulseless" inflammatory presentation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Imminent stroke risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in a critical state of severe, large-vessel vasculitis involving the aortic arch and its primary branches. The absent left radial pulse and massive blood pressure discrepancy confirm critical subclavian stenosis, while the positional visual changes and syncope indicate critically compromised blood flow to the brain (vertebrobasilar insufficiency). Urgent after-hours bedside assessment was absolutely mandated to physically measure the bilateral blood pressures, identify the specific neurological triggers, and mandate an immediate Priority 1 ambulance transfer. Leaving this patient unassessed overnight posed an unacceptable, immediate risk of a massive, devastating stroke, unequivocally justifying the MBS 585-600 service.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: The patient requires immediate, emergency transfer to the Emergency Department via 000 ambulance. The hospital must perform urgent CT/MR angiography of the aorta and cerebral vessels to define the extent of the occlusions, alongside initiating immediate, high-dose intravenous pulse corticosteroids to rapidly halt the vascular inflammation and potentially salvage blood flow before irreversible stroke occurs. Vascular surgery consultation is also required.
Positive Behavioral Change: Strict cervical immobilization: Instruct the patient to keep their head perfectly straight and use their eyes to look around, or turn their entire torso slowly; explicitly linking this absolute restriction on head-turning to preventing the physical kinking of the already narrowed blood vessels supplying the brain, directly preventing a stroke while waiting for the ambulance. Limb rest: Keep the left arm completely rested in the lap; explaining that moving the arm demands oxygen that the blocked artery cannot supply, and resting it immediately stops the claudication pain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None; critical care admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched (Priority 1). If the patient suddenly develops slurred speech, facial drooping, right-sided weakness, or loses consciousness, the spouse must immediately update 000, as this indicates a major stroke has occurred.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The complete lack of a pulse in your left arm and the dizziness when you turn your head indicate a critical inflammation and narrowing of the main blood vessels leaving your heart. Immediate emergency hospital intervention is vital to prevent a stroke.
Clinical Course: With massive doses of intravenous steroids, the inflammation in the artery walls can be rapidly reduced, hopefully restoring adequate blood flow. Some patients eventually require surgical bypass of the blocked arteries.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The ambulance is on its way. You must keep your head and neck perfectly still and straight. Do not turn your head to look at anyone. If your vision suddenly goes black, or if you feel weak on one side of your body, tell the paramedics immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is aggressively attacking the massive blood vessels in your neck and chest, causing them to swell shut. This has cut off the blood supply to your left arm and is dangerously restricting the blood flow to your brain. The hospital will use powerful intravenous medications to quickly shrink this swelling and force the vessels open.
Actionable Steps: Keep your neck completely rigid. Rest your left arm entirely. Proceed directly to the emergency department with the ambulance crew.

